Mon Apr 05 16:34:01 1999  Martin Reddy  <reddy@MAGNOLIA>

	* pixmaps/: good-bye all those numbered xpm files! Now we use
 	meaningful filenames such as sri.ppm, earth1.ppm. The images are
 	provided in PPM format, but a program call ppm2c is used to
 	compile this into inline C structures (the .h files) that can be
 	included directly into an application.

	* bitmaps/: darpa.xbm, sri.xbm, magic.xbm, tv.xbm, and icon.xbm
 	are now X11 bitmap versions of the .ppm files of the same base
 	name in the pixmaps/ directory. These are used as fallback images
 	if we can't display the colour ones.
	
	* TerraVision.c: removed workingDialog global
	(TerraVisionAnimatePlanet): [removed] bye-bye yeah olde routine
 	for animating the globe using X

	* Dialogs.c (CreateWorkingDialog): [removed] Yeah baby!!!
	(MakeIconsCB): [removed] Shagadelic!!

	* DatabaseOpts.c (ShowWorkingDialog): [removed] this used to do A
 	Bad Thing.
	(HideWorkingDialog): [removed] the counterpart of that Bad Thing.
	(DBaseConnectCallback): [removed] no longer needed removed
 	new_working_label and tsmDone globals.
	(DBaseReadServerDone): [new func] this function is called after a
 	server re-read operation has been performed.
	(DBaseReadServerThread): restructured this function to be a work
 	thread using the new BusyDialog routines. Previously, the rotating
 	globe widget used to be really flakey when re-reading a dataset
 	(due to some Bad Things that I was doing). Thing are now All Good.

	* TerraVision.c (TVLoadCallback): [deleted] this was the old
 	kludgey callback for loading a dataset with demFlag and tsmPrep
 	globals. It now uses my nice new BusyDialog routines.
	(TVDataSetLoadThread): [new func] this is the work thread that
 	loads the new dataset now. It is called via an OpenBusyDialog call
 	in TVDataSetLoad().
	(TVDataSetLoadSuccess): [new func] these are the operations that
 	are performed when a new dataset has been successfully loaded.
	(TVDataSetLoadFailure): [new func] these are the operations that
 	are performed when a new dataset fails to load (show error msg)
	(TVDataSetPrepare): [remove] part of the old cruft
	tsmPrep and demFlag globals removed - no longer needed.

	* Features.c (FeatureUnload): display a rotating-globe busy dialog
 	while loading a VRML feature file.

	* BusyDialog.c: [new file] contains lots of cool new functions for
 	managing multiple busy dialogs (with rotating globe
 	animations). The animation is done using OpenGL, rather than X,
 	and there is now no setup delay in opening the window (previously,
 	it took about 4 seconds to open the dialog on a dual
 	P400!!). Whereas before this type of dialog was hardcoded to
 	loading datasets, these new functions let you easily apply a
 	rotating globe to any lengthy operation and provide helpful funcs
 	for communicating between the parent and the worker thread, and
 	for cleaning up when all is done.

	* BusyDialog.h: [new file] definitions to support BusyDialog.c
 
	* Dialogs.c (GenericErrorDialog): if XtParent(shell) returns NULL,
 	then use shell itself (i.e. could be the applicationShell).
	(GenericMessageDialog): ditto

	* configure.in (LIBS): removed compile/link flags for the Xpm
 	library - we no longer need this library. It can be removed from
 	the source tree.

	* mo.h: [removed] anything useful moved into TerraVisionInc.h

	* pixmaps.c: removed all NCSA Mosaic source code. removed all
 	dependency upon the Xpm library. Added standard TerraVision
 	copyright header.
	(MakeColourPixmap): [new func] makes a colour pixmap from an
 	inlined 0BGR representation of an image. This now uses only raw
 	X11 calls - no dependency on the Xpm library!
	(MakeBWPixmap): [new func] makes a black and white pixmap from an
 	inlined X11 bitmap. Again, straight X11 calls.
	(MakePixmap): [new func] this is the one that calls the above two
 	functions. First we try to make a colour pixmap, but if that fails
 	(e.g. not 24-bit display), then we use a black and white image.

	* pixmaps.h: removed all the NCSA Mosaic headers. Added standard
 	TerraVision headers. Moved the pixmap/bitmap includes into
 	pixmaps.c. Moved all web icon related definitions into here.

